# /etc/synguard/rules.d/40-enforce.rules.example
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# THIS FILE DOES NOTHING. It is a template, not a policy.
#
# synguard loads only files ending in `.rules`. This one ends in `.example`,
# so it is parsed by nobody. To arm any of it:
#
#     sudo cp /etc/synguard/rules.d/40-enforce.rules.example \
#             /etc/synguard/rules.d/40-enforce.rules
#     sudo $EDITOR /etc/synguard/rules.d/40-enforce.rules   # uncomment what you want
#     sudo systemctl reload synguard
#
# Copying it as-is arms NOTHING — every rule below is commented out. That is
# deliberate: turning on a rule that kills processes should be a thing you did
# on purpose, one rule at a time.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# WHY THE STOCK POLICY HAS NO DENY RULES
#
# SynapseOS ships 55 rules and not one of them carries `deny` or `quarantine`.
# The daemon runs with --mode enforce, so acting is *permitted*; nothing loaded
# ever *asks* for it. synguard as installed detects and alerts. It does not
# kill. The startup banner says so in as many words.
#
# That is a deliberate default for a desktop distro, for two reasons.
#
# 1. MOST ENFORCEMENT HERE IS AFTER THE FACT, NOT PREVENTION.
#
#    There are now TWO paths, and which one a rule gets decides what it can
#    actually do. Read this before writing anything.
#
#    THE USERSPACE PATH — always present, always after the fact.
#
#        syscall runs and SUCCEEDS
#          → kmod appends a record to /sys/kernel/synapse/syscall_log
#            → synguard drains the ring (poll_interval_ms, default 100ms)
#              → rules match, possibly an AI round-trip
#                → SIGKILL
#
#    synapse_kmod observes with kprobe pre-handlers. It does not implement an
#    LSM hook and never returns -EPERM, so it cannot refuse anything. By the
#    time a process is killed for reading /etc/shadow, it has read /etc/shadow.
#    A deny rule buys you "it does not get to continue" — real value against
#    persistence, C2 beacons and staged payloads, and no value at all as a way
#    to stop the first act. Write rules with that in mind: target the things
#    whose damage is ongoing, not instantaneous.
#
#    THE KERNEL PATH — real prevention, but only for some rules, and only if
#    you arm it.
#
#        open() enters the kernel
#          → BPF-LSM hook evaluates the rule ON THE CALLING THREAD
#            → open() returns -EPERM. The read never happens.
#
#    No 100ms window, no pid-reuse race, no AI. A rule on this path stops the
#    first act rather than punishing it. Four things must all be true:
#
#      a. `event open`. The hook is lsm/file_open. Rules on exec, socket,
#         ptrace, module, mount and setuid stay on the userspace path — there
#         is no hook for them yet.
#      b. The rule's patterns must LOWER, and
#      c. no earlier rule may pre-empt it — both under WHICH RULES CAN BE
#         ENFORCED IN THE KERNEL, below.
#      d. synguard must run with --bpf-enforce. It is NOT in the shipped unit.
#
#    Without (d) the rule is still compiled and loaded into the kernel maps —
#    the daemon will say so — but the gate stays shut and the rule is enforced
#    only after the fact, by the userspace path. Arming is deliberate because
#    prevention is deliberate: a rule that merely kills late is recoverable,
#    and one that refuses a syscall your login needs is not.
#
#    To arm it, add --bpf-enforce to ExecStart:
#        sudo systemctl edit synguard
#        [Service]
#        ExecStart=
#        ExecStart=/usr/bin/synguard --foreground --mode enforce \
#            --rules /etc/synguard/rules.d/ --bpf-enforce
#
#    THE WAY OUT, if an armed rule locks you out of your own machine: add
#
#        synapse.bpf_enforce=0
#
#    to the kernel command line at the boot menu. synguard then never loads the
#    BPF object at all, and comes up detect-only. Do this before reaching for a
#    live USB. The gate also fails open on its own if synguard dies or wedges,
#    and refuses to act for the first 30 seconds after it attaches, so you get
#    a login even when a rule is badly wrong.
#
# 2. A FALSE POSITIVE COSTS A PROCESS TREE.
#
#    sg_kill_tree() kills the matched process AND ITS DESCENDANTS. Match a
#    shell and you take its children with it; match something in the session
#    startup chain and you can log a user out. sg_is_protected() refuses PID
#    0/1, kernel threads, synguard, synapd, synnet and synsh — that is all it
#    refuses. Your desktop, your editor and your browser are not protected.
#
# WHICH RULES CAN BE ENFORCED IN THE KERNEL
#
# A BPF program gets map lookups and bounded string work — it has no fnmatch.
# So synguard COMPILES each deny rule down to an exact string or a prefix, and
# refuses anything it cannot express exactly. It never approximates, because
# both directions of approximation are bugs you would not find: too narrow and
# the rule quietly under-enforces, too broad and it refuses things your policy
# permits.
#
# A refusal is not silent. synguard names the rule at startup and carries on
# with the userspace path:
#
#     bpf-lsm: policy NOT loaded — rule 'deny-tmp-scripts': path pattern
#     cannot be expressed in-kernel: "/tmp/*.sh"; kernel enforcement is off,
#     the userspace path is unaffected
#
# ENFORCEABLE:
#     path /etc/ld.so.preload      an exact path, no wildcard
#     path /etc/profile.d/*        a directory, one level deep
#     comm sshd                    an exact comm
#     comm python*                 a comm prefix
#
# NOT ENFORCEABLE (userspace only):
#     path /tmp/*.sh               `*` anywhere but the very end
#     path /etc/*/shadow           same
#     path /dev/input/event?       `?` and [...] have no lowered form
#     path /etc/sha*               a prefix that does not end at a '/' — the
#                                  matcher does exact and directory lookups,
#                                  so this one needs work synguard has not
#                                  done yet rather than being impossible
#     (no path at all)             a deny rule with no path would match every
#                                  open on the system
#
# Note that `/etc/profile.d/*` covers /etc/profile.d/x and NOT
# /etc/profile.d/sub/x. That is FNM_PATHNAME — `*` stops at a '/' — and the
# kernel matcher reproduces it exactly, so a directory rule means one level in
# both paths. If you want deeper, write the deeper rule too.
#
# ORDERING ALSO HAS TO LOWER, AND THIS IS THE PART THAT WILL BITE YOU.
#
# Rules are first-match-wins, so an earlier `allow` beats a later `deny`. The
# kernel only knows the rules it was handed, so a deny rule is enforceable only
# when no earlier rule could match the same input, and synguard refuses when it
# cannot PROVE they are disjoint.
#
# 00-base.rules opens with `allow-synguard` and `allow-synapd` at priority 1,
# and both are `comm <name>` with NO path — they match any event on any file.
# Nothing can be proved disjoint from that. So a deny rule written at the
# priorities suggested further down this file is refused outright:
#
#     rule deny-ld-preload-write { event open; path /etc/ld.so.preload;
#                                  verdict deny; priority 10 }
#     → bpf-lsm: policy NOT loaded — rule 'deny-ld-preload-write': an earlier
#       rule may pre-empt it (rule 'allow-synguard' comes first)
#
# The same rule at `priority 0` lowers and arms cleanly, because it now comes
# before every allow.
#
# THAT IS A REAL TRADE, NOT A TRICK. Below the allows, your rule is enforced
# late but synguard and synapd are exempt from it. Above them, it is enforced
# in the kernel and it applies to synguard and synapd too. For a file neither
# daemon touches — /etc/ld.so.preload, a udev rules directory — that is fine
# and priority 0 is right. For anything they might legitimately open, putting a
# deny above the self-exemption can make synguard kill its own supporting cast,
# so leave it below the allows and accept post-hoc enforcement.
#
# Note this makes the advice further down ("keep these numbers well above
# them") correct for the userspace path and wrong for the kernel path. There is
# no single right answer; pick per rule, based on whether the daemons touch the
# thing you are protecting.
#
# The conservatism is on purpose. The alternative is two engines that disagree
# about the same policy file, with nothing at runtime to tell you which one you
# are looking at.
#
# HOW TO CHECK WHAT IS ACTUALLY ARMED
#
#     journalctl -u synguard | grep bpf-lsm
#
#     bpf-lsm: attached · 2 rules armed · gate OPEN (armed)
#     bpf-lsm: denied=0 budget=64 opens[switch=0 warmup=0 heartbeat=0 budget=0] longpath=0
#
# `gate CLOSED` tells you why in the same line: not armed, warming up, daemon
# heartbeat stale, or deny budget spent. The opens[] counters say how often the
# gate declined to act for each of those reasons — non-zero heartbeat= means
# real denials were dropped because synguard was wedged. `longpath=` counts
# opens whose path was too long to evaluate (over 256 bytes); those are not
# enforced in the kernel, and the number is there so that gap is visible
# rather than silent.
#
# The gate also disarms ITSELF after 64 denials, so a rule that matches far
# more than you intended stops making the machine unusable. Refilled each time
# synguard reports the spend.
#
# HOW TO ROLL OUT A DENY RULE WITHOUT REGRETTING IT
#
#   a. Write it with `verdict alert` first and leave it for a few days.
#      Every alert it produces is a process it WOULD have killed.
#         journalctl -u synguard | grep <your-rule-name>
#   b. Only when that log is empty of things you recognise, switch the verdict
#      to `deny`.
#   c. Or run the whole daemon in `--mode audit` for a while: deny rules then
#      log `WOULD-DENY:` lines and kill nothing. Same for `WOULD-QUARANTINE:`.
#
# Prefer `quarantine` to `deny` when you want to keep the evidence: it SIGSTOPs
# the subtree and freezes it in a cgroup instead of killing it, so you can
# attach a debugger, read /proc/<pid>/, and then resume or kill it yourself.
#
# Rules are matched in priority order, lowest number first, and the FIRST match
# wins. The allow-rules in 00-base.rules use priority 1 so they beat anything
# here — keep these numbers well above them, UNLESS you want the rule enforced
# in the kernel, in which case it has to come before them and the trade under
# "ORDERING ALSO HAS TO LOWER" applies. Every rule below is written for the
# userspace path; move one to priority 0 only after reading that section.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────


# ── /etc/ld.so.preload ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The classic userland-rootkit hook: anything listed there is loaded into every
# dynamically linked process that starts afterwards. Nothing on a normal Arch
# system writes this file — there is no package that manages it — so a write is
# close to unambiguous, and the damage is ongoing rather than instantaneous
# (every subsequent exec inherits it), which is exactly the shape a post-hoc
# kill can still do something about.
#
# Verify before arming:  test -e /etc/ld.so.preload && cat /etc/ld.so.preload
# (on a clean system it does not exist at all)
#
# rule deny-ld-preload-write {
#     event    open
#     path     /etc/ld.so.preload
#     verdict  deny
#     priority 10
# }


# ── Execution out of /dev ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# /dev is a devtmpfs of device nodes. A regular executable file there is not a
# thing a working system produces, and running one is a well-worn way to stage
# a payload somewhere nobody audits. 00-base.rules already escalates this to
# the AI classifier; this promotes it to an outright kill.
#
# Quarantine rather than deny is suggested here: if this ever fires you will
# want to look at the process rather than lose it.
#
# `path` is matched with FNM_PATHNAME, so `*` stops at a `/`: the first rule
# covers /dev/foo and NOT /dev/shm/foo. /dev/shm is the more likely staging
# ground of the two, so it needs its own line — a one-level glob here is a
# common way to think you have covered /dev when you have not.
#
# rule quarantine-exec-from-dev {
#     event    exec
#     path     /dev/*
#     verdict  quarantine
#     priority 12
# }
#
# rule quarantine-exec-from-dev-subdir {
#     event    exec
#     path     /dev/*/*
#     verdict  quarantine
#     priority 12
# }


# ── Kernel module loads by an unexpected program ─────────────────────────────
# 00-base.rules allows modprobe, udev and the kmod tooling by name and alerts
# on everything else. A module load from anything else is either a packaging
# bug or someone loading a rootkit.
#
# READ THIS BEFORE ARMING: DKMS rebuilds, nvidia driver updates, VirtualBox,
# VMware and anything else that ships out-of-tree modules will load modules
# from programs not on the allow-list. Run it as `alert` across at least one
# full system update and one reboot before you trust it.
#
# rule deny-unexpected-module-load {
#     event    module
#     verdict  deny
#     priority 20
# }


# ── Shell spawned by a network-facing service ────────────────────────────────
# The reverse-shell signature. This is the highest-value rule in this file and
# also the easiest to get wrong: `comm` matches the process that made the call,
# and plenty of legitimate daemons spawn shells (cron jobs, hooks, CGI,
# systemd's ExecStartPre, package scriptlets, sudo).
#
# Do NOT arm this without tailoring the pattern to services you actually run
# and watching it in `alert` for a week. It is included because it is the rule
# people come here wanting, and shipping it commented with this warning is
# safer than leaving everyone to write it from scratch.
#
# One rule per service: `comm` is matched with fnmatch(3), which has globs but
# NO alternation. `comm nginx|httpd|sshd` is not a pattern that matches three
# names — it is a literal string that matches nothing, and a rule that can
# never fire looks identical to a rule that never needed to.
#
# rule deny-shell-from-nginx {
#     event    exec
#     comm     nginx
#     path     /usr/bin/sh
#     verdict  deny
#     priority 15
# }
#
# rule deny-shell-from-sshd {
#     event    exec
#     comm     sshd
#     path     /usr/bin/sh
#     verdict  deny
#     priority 15
# }


# ── Your own rules go below ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Full syntax reference: /etc/synguard/rules.d/00-base.rules
#
#   event    exec | open | socket | ptrace | module | mount | setuid
#   uid      <numeric uid>, omit for any
#   comm     fnmatch pattern against the process name (max 15 chars — the
#            kernel truncates comm, so "systemd-userwork" is "systemd-userwor")
#   path     fnmatch pattern against the file or target
#   verdict  allow | log | alert | escalate | deny | quarantine
#   priority lower number = matched first; first match wins
